C0877 | divinity-final-reading-scenes-v7/reader-body/baptism-appointed/reading.text ?? description
Aquinas believes God chose baptism with water to give inward grace. The water acts through God's choice; it does not gain independent divine power.

C0878 | divinity-final-reading-scenes-v7/tile/reader-caption/instrumental-baptism/label; divinity-final-reading-scenes-v7/wonder italic caption/plain-divinity-instrumental-baptism/item.label
Aquinas: in baptism, God works through water to give inward grace

C0879 | divinity-final-reading-scenes-v7/reader-body/instrumental-baptism/reading.text ?? description; divinity-final-reading-scenes-v7/wonder body/plain-divinity-instrumental-baptism/text
For Aquinas, baptism washes the body while God works through the rite to give inward grace. The water is God's instrument, not a second god. This depends on his further belief that God appointed baptism; ordinary water is not automatically a sacrament.

C0880 | divinity-final-reading-scenes-v7/tile/reader-caption/help-not-erasure/label; divinity-final-reading-scenes-v7/wonder italic caption/plain-divinity-help-not-erasure/item.label
God’s help need not stop a person from really choosing

C0881 | divinity-final-reading-scenes-v7/reader-body/help-not-erasure/reading.text ?? description
Augustine's helped person still chooses, and Aquinas's tool still acts. The comparison shows that dependent action can be real; it does not mean people are merely tools.

C0882 | divinity-final-reading-scenes-v7/tile/reader-caption/rites-different/label; divinity-final-reading-scenes-v7/wonder italic caption/plain-divinity-rites-different/item.label
Both rites use bodies; they disagree about what God appointed

C0883 | divinity-final-reading-scenes-v7/reader-body/rites-different/reading.text ?? description; divinity-final-reading-scenes-v7/wonder body/plain-divinity-rites-different/text
The Lyon rite uses hands and a Gospel book; Aquinas discusses baptism with water. Both claim an inward gift through physical actions, but disagree about the authorized rite and how it gives that gift.

C0884 | divinity-final-reading-scenes-v7/tile/reader-caption/bread-taste/label
Case: after the church’s blessing, it still looks and tastes like bread

C0885 | divinity-final-reading-scenes-v7/reader-body/bread-taste/reading.text ?? description
After the blessing, the bread looks and tastes the same. Aquinas's account must explain why he still says that what it is has changed.

C0886 | divinity-final-reading-scenes-v7/tile/reader-caption/supper-words/label
Jesus: “This is my body”

C0887 | divinity-final-reading-scenes-v7/reader-body/supper-words/reading.text ?? description
Christians dispute what these supper words mean. Aquinas reads them as affirming the real presence of Christ’s body, not only a reminder.

C0888 | divinity-final-reading-scenes-v7/tile/reader-caption/aquinas-change/label
Aquinas: what was bread becomes Christ’s body

C0889 | divinity-final-reading-scenes-v7/reader-body/aquinas-change/reading.text ?? description
Aquinas says the bread becomes Christ’s body, although its taste, colour and shape remain. He is not saying that it starts to look or taste like flesh.

This claim is called transubstantiation: a change in what the thing is, while its appearances remain. Other Christians dispute the claim.

C0890 | divinity-final-reading-scenes-v7/tile/reader-caption/appearance-rule/label
Aquinas: what a thing is can differ from how it looks and tastes

C0891 | divinity-final-reading-scenes-v7/reader-body/appearance-rule/reading.text ?? description
Aquinas distinguishes what something is from its appearance. In Communion, he says the bread's substance—what it is—changes, while qualities such as taste and colour remain. He calls those remaining qualities accidents.

C0892 | divinity-final-reading-scenes-v7/tile/reader-caption/changed-claim/label
Aquinas reads Christ’s words as changing bread into his body

C0893 | divinity-final-reading-scenes-v7/reader-body/changed-claim/reading.text ?? description
Aquinas reads Christ's supper words as saying that bread becomes Christ's body. This is a particular interpretation, not the only possible reading established by the words alone.

C0894 | divinity-final-reading-scenes-v7/tile/reader-caption/same-appearances/label
For Aquinas, unchanged taste does not mean unchanged reality

C0895 | divinity-final-reading-scenes-v7/reader-body/same-appearances/reading.text ?? description
Aquinas says the appearance stays the same while what the thing is changes. Unchanged taste therefore does not contradict his account, but it does not verify it either.

C0896 | divinity-final-reading-scenes-v7/tile/reader-caption/eucharist-account/label
Aquinas: it becomes Christ’s body but keeps bread’s look and taste

C0897 | divinity-final-reading-scenes-v7/reader-body/eucharist-account/reading.text ?? description
Aquinas says the bread becomes Christ's body while keeping the look and taste of bread. This is called transubstantiation. Explaining the claim about Communion does not establish that the change occurred.

C0898 | divinity-final-reading-scenes-v7/tile/reader-caption/not-sensory-proof/label; divinity-final-reading-scenes-v7/wonder italic caption/plain-divinity-not-sensory-proof/item.label
This explains Aquinas’s claim; tasting the bread does not prove it

C0899 | divinity-final-reading-scenes-v7/reader-body/not-sensory-proof/reading.text ?? description; divinity-final-reading-scenes-v7/wonder body/plain-divinity-not-sensory-proof/text
Tasting the bread cannot prove the change Aquinas describes, because he says its taste remains. His account rests on faith in divine authority, not on a hidden change detected by the senses.

C0900 | divinity-final-reading-scenes-v7/tile/reader-caption/two-material-roles/label; divinity-final-reading-scenes-v7/wonder italic caption/plain-divinity-two-material-roles/item.label
Aquinas: baptism’s water is God’s tool; Communion’s bread becomes Christ’s body

C0901 | divinity-final-reading-scenes-v7/reader-body/two-material-roles/reading.text ?? description; divinity-final-reading-scenes-v7/wonder body/plain-divinity-two-material-roles/text
For Aquinas, baptism's water is a tool through which God acts. In Communion, what was bread becomes Christ's body while appearances remain. These give physical things different roles.

C0902 | divinity-final-reading-scenes-v7/tile/reader-caption/single-coin/label
Case: the household has one coin and no supper

C0903 | divinity-final-reading-scenes-v7/reader-body/single-coin/reading.text ?? description
The household has one coin and no supper. Spending it on an indulgence would leave the family's immediate need for food unmet.

C0904 | divinity-final-reading-scenes-v7/tile/reader-caption/seller-guarantee/label
Seller’s claim reported by Luther: a coin frees a dead soul

C0905 | divinity-final-reading-scenes-v7/reader-body/seller-guarantee/reading.text ?? description
Luther reports sellers promising that payment releases a dead person from punishment. He attacks the claim that a coin can guarantee that result.

The punishment in question is purgatory: purification after death for people ultimately saved, not hell. A reported sales pitch is not the same as official doctrine.

C0906 | divinity-final-reading-scenes-v7/tile/reader-caption/letter-penalties/label
An indulgence concerns penalties remaining after sins are forgiven

C0907 | divinity-final-reading-scenes-v7/reader-body/letter-penalties/reading.text ?? description
In this debate, being forgiven can still leave penalties to be dealt with. An indulgence concerns those penalties; it is not supposed to buy forgiveness itself.

Luther questions what the pope can release. The seller’s promised release of a dead soul is a disputed sales claim, not an official definition of Catholic teaching.

C0908 | divinity-final-reading-scenes-v7/tile/reader-caption/care-first-rule/label
Luther: household necessities and poor neighbours come first

C0909 | divinity-final-reading-scenes-v7/reader-body/care-first-rule/reading.text ?? description
Luther gives care a positive priority, rather than treating the purchase as the only religious use for money.

C0910 | divinity-final-reading-scenes-v7/tile/reader-caption/declare-forgiveness/label
Luther: the pope announces forgiveness; God is the one who forgives

C0911 | divinity-final-reading-scenes-v7/reader-body/declare-forgiveness/reading.text ?? description
In 1517 Luther still allows a ministerial role. Declaring God’s forgiveness is not an independent power to make God forgive on receipt of money.

C0912 | divinity-final-reading-scenes-v7/tile/reader-caption/repent-whole-life/label
Luther: repentance means a lifetime of turning away from wrong

C0913 | divinity-final-reading-scenes-v7/reader-body/repent-whole-life/reading.text ?? description
Repentance is turning from wrong, not a transaction completed once and forgotten. This claim helps explain why a guarantee based on payment troubles Luther.

C0914 | divinity-final-reading-scenes-v7/tile/reader-caption/coin-not-control/label
For Luther, a seller cannot guarantee God’s mercy for a coin

C0915 | divinity-final-reading-scenes-v7/reader-body/coin-not-control/reading.text ?? description
Luther rejects a seller's guarantee that a payment will release a dead soul. A limited church role does not give the seller control over God's mercy.

C0916 | divinity-final-reading-scenes-v7/tile/reader-caption/feed-before-letter/label; divinity-final-reading-scenes-v7/wonder italic caption/plain-divinity-feed-before-letter/item.label
Luther: buy the hungry household supper before an indulgence letter

C0917 | divinity-final-reading-scenes-v7/reader-body/feed-before-letter/reading.text ?? description; divinity-final-reading-scenes-v7/wonder body/plain-divinity-feed-before-letter/text
Luther gives household necessities and help for poor neighbours priority over buying an indulgence. With one coin and no supper, the household should buy food first.

C0918 | divinity-final-reading-scenes-v7/tile/reader-caption/ministry-not-sale/label; divinity-final-reading-scenes-v7/wonder italic caption/plain-divinity-ministry-not-sale/item.label
Working for God does not mean controlling God’s gifts

C0919 | divinity-final-reading-scenes-v7/reader-body/ministry-not-sale/reading.text ?? description; divinity-final-reading-scenes-v7/wonder body/plain-divinity-ministry-not-sale/text
A person can serve God without controlling God's gifts. Comparing Luther's limits on church authority with Aquinas's dependent instrument clarifies that distinction without making their doctrines identical.

C0920 | divinity-final-reading-scenes-v7/tile/reader-caption/no-paid-shortcut/label; divinity-final-reading-scenes-v7/wonder italic caption/plain-divinity-no-paid-shortcut/item.label
Luther: a paid guarantee cannot replace a changed life

C0921 | divinity-final-reading-scenes-v7/reader-body/no-paid-shortcut/reading.text ?? description
For Luther, a guarantee based on payment cannot replace repentance and a changed life. This criticism is not a blanket claim that every church rite or payment is fraudulent.

C0922 | divinity-final-reading-scenes-v7/tile/reader-caption/gift-acceptance/label
Luther: faith (trust in Christ), not good deeds, makes us right with God

C0923 | divinity-final-reading-scenes-v7/reader-body/gift-acceptance/reading.text ?? description
Luther says faith, or trust in Christ and his promise, makes a person right with God. This is called justification. Helping a neighbour meets a real need, but does not earn God's acceptance.

C0924 | divinity-final-reading-scenes-v7/tile/reader-caption/why-help/label
Case: if God accepts me without payment, why feed anyone?

C0925 | divinity-final-reading-scenes-v7/reader-body/why-help/reading.text ?? description
A reader takes Luther's rejection of earning God's acceptance as a reason to do nothing. But a neighbour still needs help. What should good deeds accomplish?

C0926 | divinity-final-reading-scenes-v7/tile/reader-caption/neighbour-still-hungry/label
Case: the neighbour’s hunger has not vanished

C0927 | divinity-final-reading-scenes-v7/reader-body/neighbour-still-hungry/reading.text ?? description
Freedom from buying God’s acceptance does not itself supply the neighbour’s food. The case asks who a good work is for.

C0928 | divinity-final-reading-scenes-v7/tile/reader-caption/christ-serves/label
Luther: Christ served me freely; I can freely serve my neighbour

C0929 | divinity-final-reading-scenes-v7/reader-body/christ-serves/reading.text ?? description
Luther says Christians should serve their neighbours freely, as Christ served them. They help because the neighbour needs it, not to earn God's acceptance.

C0930 | divinity-final-reading-scenes-v7/tile/reader-caption/body-practice/label
Luther: train the body to serve, not to earn God’s acceptance

C0931 | divinity-final-reading-scenes-v7/reader-body/body-practice/reading.text ?? description
The text does not discard bodily life. Work and discipline remain, but their role must be distinguished from earning acceptance.

C0932 | divinity-final-reading-scenes-v7/tile/reader-caption/no-wages/label
Not earning God’s acceptance does not make helping others pointless

C0933 | divinity-final-reading-scenes-v7/reader-body/no-wages/reading.text ?? description
Feeding someone can help them even if it does not earn acceptance by God. Luther gives service a purpose in the neighbour's need rather than treating it as a payment for salvation.

C0934 | divinity-final-reading-scenes-v7/tile/reader-caption/free-service/label
Luther: feed the neighbour because they need food, not to earn salvation

C0935 | divinity-final-reading-scenes-v7/reader-body/free-service/reading.text ?? description
Luther calls for service because the neighbour needs help, following Christ's freely given service. The deed is not a price paid to earn God's acceptance.

C0936 | divinity-final-reading-scenes-v7/tile/reader-caption/care-not-purchase/label; divinity-final-reading-scenes-v7/wonder italic caption/plain-divinity-care-not-purchase/item.label
Feeding the household helps people; it does not buy acceptance by God

C0937 | divinity-final-reading-scenes-v7/reader-body/care-not-purchase/reading.text ?? description; divinity-final-reading-scenes-v7/wonder body/plain-divinity-care-not-purchase/text
The household still needs supper. For Luther, feeding them serves that need without buying acceptance by God. A good deed can matter to other people without becoming payment for salvation.

C0938 | divinity-final-reading-scenes-v7/tile/reader-caption/two-grace-questions/label; divinity-final-reading-scenes-v7/wonder italic caption/plain-divinity-two-grace-questions/item.label
Augustine: what helps me act? Luther: what makes me right with God?

C0939 | divinity-final-reading-scenes-v7/owned-location tile/reader-caption/two-grace-questions/labelByLocation @divinity-augustine-study; divinity-final-reading-scenes-v7/owned-location tile/reader-caption/two-grace-questions/labelByLocation @divinity-grace-alcove; divinity-final-reading-scenes-v7/wonder owned-location italic caption/plain-divinity-two-grace-questions/item.labelByLocation @divinity-augustine-study; divinity-final-reading-scenes-v7/wonder owned-location italic caption/plain-divinity-two-grace-questions/item.labelByLocation @divinity-grace-alcove
what helps me act? Luther: what makes me right with God?

C0940 | divinity-final-reading-scenes-v7/reader-body/two-grace-questions/reading.text ?? description; divinity-final-reading-scenes-v7/wonder body/plain-divinity-two-grace-questions/text
Augustine asks how God helps a person want and do what is right. Luther asks what makes a person right with God. Both concern God's gift, but answering one question does not fully answer the other.

C0941 | divinity-final-reading-scenes-v7/tile/reader-caption/practice-not-price/label; divinity-final-reading-scenes-v7/wonder italic caption/plain-divinity-practice-not-price/item.label
Luther: bodily discipline trains me; it does not buy God’s acceptance

C0942 | divinity-final-reading-scenes-v7/reader-body/practice-not-price/reading.text ?? description; divinity-final-reading-scenes-v7/wonder body/plain-divinity-practice-not-price/text
For Luther, bodily work and discipline can train a person for service. Effort matters without becoming the price of God's acceptance.

C0943 | divinity-final-reading-scenes-v7/tile/reader-caption/remembered-ring/label
Zwingli: a ring recalls a beloved without containing their body

C0944 | divinity-final-reading-scenes-v7/reader-body/remembered-ring/reading.text ?? description
A ring can bring a loved person to mind without containing their body. Zwingli uses this example to explain how Communion's bread can point to Christ without containing his body.

C0945 | divinity-final-reading-scenes-v7/tile/reader-caption/body-in-heaven/label
Zwingli: Christ’s human body is in heaven

C0946 | divinity-final-reading-scenes-v7/reader-body/body-in-heaven/reading.text ?? description
Zwingli argues that Christ's human body is in heaven after the Ascension. He therefore reads the Communion bread as a sign of that body, not a place where the body is located.

C0947 | divinity-final-reading-scenes-v7/tile/reader-caption/luther-word-makes/label
Luther: Christ’s words give his body with the bread

C0948 | divinity-final-reading-scenes-v7/reader-body/luther-word-makes/reading.text ?? description
In the 1529 catechism, the sacrament depends on Christ’s words, not the participant’s ability to create it by believing. This is not Aquinas’s account of changed bread.

C0949 | divinity-final-reading-scenes-v7/tile/reader-caption/unbelieving-guest/label
Case: a Communion guest eats the bread without trusting Christ’s promise

C0950 | divinity-final-reading-scenes-v7/reader-body/unbelieving-guest/reading.text ?? description
The guest tests two questions: what is received, and whether receiving it brings the promised benefit. The two need not have the same answer in Luther’s account.

C0951 | divinity-final-reading-scenes-v7/tile/reader-caption/faith-benefit/label
Luther: Communion’s promised forgiveness is received only through faith—trust in Christ’s promise

C0952 | divinity-final-reading-scenes-v7/reader-body/faith-benefit/reading.text ?? description
For Luther, the promised forgiveness in Communion is received through faith: trusting Christ's promise that the gift is for you. Merely eating does not supply that trust. He distinguishes receiving Christ's body from receiving the forgiveness that also strengthens faith.

C0953 | divinity-final-reading-scenes-v7/tile/reader-caption/signed-trinity/label
Marburg’s signers affirm one God in three persons

C0954 | divinity-final-reading-scenes-v7/reader-body/signed-trinity/reading.text ?? description
The jointly signed first article affirms the Trinity. Agreement on this matter does not remove their dispute about the supper.

C0955 | divinity-final-reading-scenes-v7/tile/reader-caption/signed-supper/label
Marburg: Christ is received through faith; his bodily presence stays disputed

C0956 | divinity-final-reading-scenes-v7/reader-body/signed-supper/reading.text ?? description
The signers affirm receiving Christ through faith and the supper's strengthening of faith. They still disagree about Christ's bodily presence in bread and wine; they do not share one account of how that presence works.

C0957 | divinity-final-reading-scenes-v7/tile/reader-caption/zwingli-reading/label
Zwingli: bread points to Christ’s body; it does not contain it